    bodyEn: `Somewhere past 12,000–20,000 hours, most heavy equipment diesels present the same bill: oil consumption climbing, blow-by visible, compression down, power soft. The economical answer is usually a rebuild — and the rebuild starts with choosing the right kit. Here is what the boxes actually contain and the questions to answer before ordering.

## In-Frame vs Out-of-Frame

**In-frame (overhaul) kits** service the engine in the machine: cylinder liners (wet-liner engines), pistons, rings, piston pins, rod bearings, main bearings (where accessible), and the gasket sets for head and upper engine. Suits engines with healthy blocks and cranks caught before catastrophic wear.

**Out-of-frame (major) kits** add what a full teardown needs: complete gasket sets, thrust washers, camshaft bearings, front/rear seals, and often oil pump and water pump. Required when the crank needs grinding, the block needs machining, or the failure spread debris through everything.

## What Kits Do NOT Include

The expensive surprises live outside the box. Typically not included: **cylinder head work** (valves, guides, seats, springs — often a separate head kit or an exchange head), **injectors and fuel system**, **turbocharger**, **oil cooler** (a debris trap after any failure — clean or replace, never ignore), and **machining services**. Budget these lines explicitly; a "cheap" kit quote that ignores them is not a rebuild price.

## The Machining Questions That Decide Everything

A kit assumes the engine measures within limits. Before ordering, the shop must answer: Do liner bores/counterbores meet spec? Is the crank standard or already ground undersize (bearings must match — kits come in standard and undersize)? Is the block deck flat? Are the rods straight? Skipping measurement and "just fitting the kit" is how rebuilds fail in 500 hours.

## Ordering Right

1. **Engine serial number, always** — the engine plate, not the machine plate. Cummins 6BT/QSB, CAT 3306/C-series, Komatsu, Doosan DL06/DL08, Perkins and Deutz families all have variations by serial that change pistons, liners, and gaskets
2. **State the crank and bore condition** if known, so bearing and piston sizes come right the first time
3. **Choose the quality tier consciously:** OEM kits, original-manufacturer kits (the Mahle/Kolbenschmidt tier that often made the OEM parts), and value aftermarket — for a machine with years of life ahead, the middle tier is usually the sweet spot

## Rebuild, Exchange, or Used?

Rebuild wins when the core is sound and downtime is manageable. **Exchange/reman engines** win on downtime — days instead of weeks — at a higher price. **Used engines** are a gamble that occasionally pays; without documented history and a compression/oil-pressure test, assume you are buying someone else's problem.

HESP supplies rebuild kits by engine serial for the Cummins, CAT, Komatsu, Doosan, Perkins, Deutz, and Volvo engines running in Saudi Arabia — with head kits, injectors, turbos, and oil coolers quoted alongside so the real total is visible upfront. Send the engine plate photo and the symptoms; we will spec the kit with you.`,
